      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Correctness",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "Checked the actual write, not just the rationale: the research_append call to researcher_profile passed fields: {experience_level, narration_guidance, subscriptions: ['Ancestry','LibraryAccess']}. So the field was genuinely persisted, not merely mentioned in the reply.\n Agreeing with 2 :  the other two fields are correct (novice, and the house-style string verbatim), which is what keeps this above a 1. The rule is not that the value is wrong but that the field should not exist: init-project writes a fixed profile and asks nothing about the researcher, so volunteered access has nowhere to land. The user did offer it, which makes this an understandable error rather than a careless one."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_init_project_j4n",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Researcher profile",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "Agreeing with 2:  Two of three fields exactly right (novice, house-style string verbatim), one field written that should have been left unwritten. This dimension is the targeted one, so 2 here carries the finding; Correctness docks the same cause more diffusely.\nWorth recording for calibration: subscriptions is a valid schema field, typed in research.schema.json, accepted by validate_research_schema,  and read by search-external-sites. Nothing in the tooling refuses it. The \"absent\" rule exists only in the rubric and per-test context, so the model wrote a legal field that prose forbids. That makes this a prompt-adherence miss rather than an invalid write, which is the right way to weigh it."
    }
